{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] explicitly states that the Write-Ahead Log (WAL) option became available beginning with version 3.7.0.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "Beginning with version 3.7.0 (2010-07-21), a new \"Write-Ahead Log\" option (hereafter referred to as \"WAL\") is available."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that it is not possible to change the page_size after entering WAL mode, even by restoring from a backup or using VACUUM. Passage [2] confirms that closing and reopening keeps the database in WAL mode. Therefore, simply closing and reopening does not allow changing the page size because the mode remains WAL.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "It is not possible to change the page_size after entering WAL mode..."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "If a process sets WAL mode, then closes and reopens the database, the database will come back in WAL mode."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] explicitly states: 'You must be in a rollback journal mode to change the page size.' This implies switching out of WAL mode (which is not a rollback journal mode in this context, or rather, you must leave WAL to enter a rollback mode) is required.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "You must be in a rollback journal mode to change the page size."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] establishes WAL appeared in 3.7.0. Passage [3] states that older versions could not read a WAL-mode database without write access, and this constraint was relaxed beginning with version 3.22.0. Thus, between 3.7.0 and 3.22.0, write access was required for reading.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "Beginning with version 3.7.0 (2010-07-21), a new \"Write-Ahead Log\" option... is available."
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Older versions of SQLite could not read a WAL-mode database that was read-only. In other words, write access was required in order to read a WAL-mode database. This constraint was relaxed beginning with SQLite version 3.22.0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "None of the provided passages mention the default page size value (e.g., 4096 bytes).",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] states that if the conversion to WAL could not be completed, for example if the VFS does not support necessary shared-memory primitives, the journaling mode will be unchanged.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "If the conversion to WAL could not be completed (for example, if the VFS does not support the necessary shared-memory primitives) then the journaling mode will be unchanged..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] states that if the conversion fails, the pragma returns the prior journaling mode string (e.g., 'delete'), not an error.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "...then the journaling mode will be unchanged and the string returned from the primitive will be the prior journaling mode (for example \"delete\")."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] lists three specific policies: `appendfsync always`, `appendfsync everysec`, and `appendfsync no`. Passage [2] also mentions these three options.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "* `appendfsync always`: ... * `appendfsync everysec`: ... * `appendfsync no`: ..."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "you can have different fsync policies: no fsync at all, fsync every second, fsync at every query."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] explicitly states that for `appendfsync always`, `fsync` is called every time new commands are appended to the AOF.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "`appendfsync always`: `fsync` every time new commands are appended to the AOF."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] describes `appendfsync always` as \"Very very slow, very safe.\" Passage [1] confirms it is \"very slow in practice.\"",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "Very very slow, very safe."
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "The `always` policy is very slow in practice"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] states that the suggested and default policy is to `fsync` every second (`everysec`). Passage [2] also refers to the default policy of fsync every second. The claim says the default is `no`, which contradicts the text.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "The suggested (and default) policy is to `fsync` every second."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "With the default policy of fsync every second"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that with `appendfsync no`, \"Normally Linux will flush data every 30 seconds with this configuration\".",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "Normally Linux will flush data every 30 seconds with this configuration"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [0] states that with `appendfsync everysec`, \"you may lose 1 second of data if there is a disaster.\" Passage [2] reiterates that you can only lose one second worth of writes.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "you may lose 1 second of data if there is a disaster."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "so you can only lose one second worth of writes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "While Passage [2] mentions that fsync is performed using a background thread and the main thread tries hard to perform writes when no fsync is in progress, it does not state that the main thread *never* has to wait for disk I/O. It implies contention or waiting might still occur ('tries hard'), but doesn't guarantee zero waiting. More importantly, the specific claim 'never has to wait' is an absolute statement not fully entailed by the text which focuses on minimizing loss rather than eliminating all I/O waits for the main thread in all scenarios.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages describe `appendfsync always` as \"Very very slow\" and \"very slow in practice\", but they do not provide quantitative metrics regarding write throughput reduction (e.g., \"cuts throughput by more than half\"). This specific numerical claim is not present in the source text.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
